    "category": "Primary + Backup Combo",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Primary and 12-volt backup pump in one pre-assembled unit",
      "Single discharge simplifies installation in an existing pit",
      "Backup controller includes an alarm for pump or power failure",
      "Covers the most common failure scenario, a power outage in a storm",
      "Widely sold with replacement parts available"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Battery sold separately in many listings",
      "Combo assembly needs a pit wide enough to fit it",
      "Backup battery needs periodic replacement"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The easiest way to give an existing sump pit both a primary pump and a battery backup in one installation.",
    "description": "The Wayne WSS30V pairs a 1/2 horsepower primary sump pump with a 12-volt battery backup pump, pre-plumbed into a single assembly with one discharge. That arrangement removes most of the fiddly work of fitting two pumps and two check valves into one pit, which is where many DIY backup installations go wrong. When the primary stops for any reason, whether a failed switch, a jammed float or a power outage, the backup kicks in and the controller sounds an alarm. Wayne lists the system for typical residential pits; measure yours before ordering, because the combined unit is wider than a single pump. The backup runs from a deep-cycle battery that you buy separately in many listings, and its run time depends on battery size and how often the pump cycles. Owners commonly report straightforward installation in existing pits and appreciate the alarm. Like any backup, it needs testing a few times a year. At the time of writing it typically sells for roughly $350 to $500 without the battery."
  },
  {
    "id": "zoeller-m53-mighty-mate",
    "name": "Zoeller M53 Mighty-Mate Submersible Sump Pump",
    "category": "Primary Sump Pump",
    "badge": "Best Primary Pump",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Cast-iron housing dissipates motor heat",
      "Mechanical float switch with a long track record",
      "Sized at 1/3 horsepower, right for most homes",
      "Parts and service widely available through plumbing suppliers",
      "Fits standard residential pits"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No backup or alarm included",
      "Heavier than plastic pumps",
      "Float switch is integrated, so a switch failure means service or replacement"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A durable, proven primary pump that plumbers have installed for decades.",
    "description": "The Zoeller M53 is about as close to a standard as residential sump pumps get. It is a 1/3 horsepower submersible pump with a cast-iron motor housing and a mechanical float switch, and it is the model many plumbers reach for when replacing a failed pump. The cast-iron body helps shed motor heat into the surrounding water, which supports long service life when the pump runs often. The integrated float is compact enough for typical 18 to 24 inch pits. Zoeller publishes performance curves for the M53, and for most homes with modest discharge height it moves plenty of water; homes with very high water tables or long discharge runs should check the curve or step up to a larger model. The M53 does not include a backup pump or alarm, so pair it with a separate battery backup or at least a high-water alarm. Owners commonly report many years of service, and it is easy to find through plumbing supply houses. Expect roughly $170 to $250 at the time of writing."
  },
  {
    "id": "basement-watchdog-special-bwsp",
    "name": "Basement Watchdog Special Battery Backup Sump Pump System",
    "category": "Add-On Battery Backup",
    "badge": "Best Backup Add-On",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Adds a backup to an existing primary pump",
      "Controller with audible alarm and battery monitoring",
      "Dual float switch design for reliability",
      "Widely available at home centers",
      "Expandable with a second battery for longer run time"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Battery typically sold separately",
      "Needs its own check valve and discharge connection",
      "Controller and battery take up shelf space beside the pit"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A good way to add battery protection to a pit that already has a pump you trust.",
    "description": "If you already have a solid primary pump, the Basement Watchdog Special adds battery protection without replacing it. The system includes a 12-volt pump that sits in the pit above the primary, a float switch assembly, and a controller that charges the battery, monitors it and sounds an alarm when the backup runs or when the battery needs attention. Basement Watchdog uses a two-float switch arrangement intended to reduce the chance of switch failure, and the controller can support a second battery for longer run time during extended outages. Installation means teeing the backup into the existing discharge with its own check valve, a job most handy owners can finish in an afternoon. The battery is usually purchased separately; a deep-cycle AGM type reduces maintenance. Owners commonly report that the alarm alone has saved them from flooded basements by warning of a stuck primary. At the time of writing, the system usually costs roughly $200 to $300 before the battery."
  },
  {
    "id": "radonseal-crack-injection-kit",
    "name": "RadonSeal Foundation Crack Repair Kit",
    "category": "Polyurethane Crack Injection",
    "badge": "Best Crack Repair",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Polyurethane foam expands to fill the crack through the wall",
      "Includes ports, surface seal and injection materials",
      "Designed for homeowner use without special equipment",
      "Far cheaper than a contractor visit for one crack"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Best suited to poured concrete, not hollow block",
      "Requires careful surface prep and patience",
      "Not a fix for structural cracks"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A homeowner-friendly way to permanently seal a leaking shrinkage crack in a poured concrete wall.",
    "description": "A single leaking crack does not need a drainage system, and this kit is how most owners can fix one themselves. It follows the professional method in a homeowner package: injection ports are glued along the crack, the face of the crack is sealed with a surface paste, and a polyurethane resin is injected from the lowest port upward so it fills the crack through the thickness of the wall. Polyurethane expands as it cures and remains slightly flexible, which helps it tolerate the small seasonal movement shrinkage cracks see. The maker sizes kits by crack length, so measure first. Success depends on preparation: the surface paste needs a clean, dry face to bond, and injecting too fast can push resin out of the ports instead of into the crack. The kit is meant for poured concrete; hollow block walls let resin escape into the cores. It is not appropriate for horizontal or widening cracks, which need structural evaluation. At the time of writing, kits run roughly $80 to $150."
  },
