      "Must go on bare, clean masonry",
      "Cannot stop water at the floor joint",
      "Labor-intensive brush application"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The barrier layer for damp interior walls, best used after outdoor drainage is fixed.",
    "description": "In a waterproofing system, DRYLOK Extreme fills the barrier role from inside. It is a thick masonry waterproofer that the manufacturer rates to withstand 15 psi of hydrostatic pressure when applied according to the label: two coats on bare, clean masonry, with the first coat scrubbed in by brush to fill every pore. On a block wall that seeps after storms, or a poured wall with a persistent damp band, it is often the only interior measure needed once gutters and grading are handled. Its limits define where it fits in a system. It cannot reach water entering along the seam between wall and floor, it does not stop water rising through the slab, and it will not hold back a flowing crack. Paired with an interior drain, it keeps the visible part of the wall drier and reduces the load on the membrane. Paired with nothing, on a wall with water at the floor joint, it will look like a failure. Expect roughly $40 to $60 a gallon at the time of writing."
  },
  {
    "id": "liquid-rubber-foundation",
    "name": "Liquid Rubber Foundation & Basement Sealant",
    "category": "DIY Exterior Membrane",
    "badge": "Best Exterior DIY Membrane",
