    "bottomLine": "A practical membrane for a partial excavation or one exposed wall when you are doing the digging yourself.",
    "description": "When a single wall is dug out, for a downspout repair, a new window well or a partial excavation on the leaking side, Liquid Rubber Foundation and Basement Sealant is a DIY-friendly way to add a real membrane while the concrete is exposed. It is a water-based elastomeric coating that cures into a seamless, flexible skin, able to bridge hairline cracks as the wall moves with the seasons. On the exterior, water pressure pushes the membrane into the wall rather than away from it, which is the ideal arrangement for any coating. The maker specifies building it up in multiple coats to reach the recommended thickness, and skimping on thickness forfeits most of the benefit. As a system part it is only one layer: protect it with drainage board before backfilling, and make sure the footing has a working drain so water does not simply sit against the membrane. Never dig beside a foundation deeper than a few feet without proper shoring. At the time of writing, expect roughly $100 to $150 a gallon."
