    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Contractors sell basement waterproofing as a branded system with a warranty and a price tag, but every system is built from the same few parts. Knowing what those parts do lets you judge a quote, skip what you do not need, and buy the pieces you can install yourself. The best system is the smallest one that keeps your particular basement dry.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Waterproofing System Components, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These picks are the components homeowners actually buy or specify for a waterproofing system: pumps, backups, crack repair and coatings. Rankings weigh published specifications (motor size, construction, switch type, pressure ratings), warranty terms, how widely parts and service are available, and recurring patterns in owner feedback. We did not run these products ourselves; specifications come from the manufacturers.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="parts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a Basement Waterproofing System Actually Includes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Strip away the brand names and nearly every system is assembled from four kinds of parts. <strong>Barriers</strong> keep water off or out of the concrete: exterior membranes, interior coatings, penetrating sealers. <strong>Collectors</strong> catch water that gets past the barrier: perimeter drain tile, wall membranes, channel drains. <strong>Movers</strong> get collected water out of the house: sump pumps, backups, discharge lines. And <strong>repairs</strong> close specific holes: crack injection, hydraulic cement, pipe seals.
          </p>

          <p>
            A full professional interior system uses a collector, a mover and often a dimpled wall membrane that acts as a partial barrier. A full exterior system uses a barrier and a collector outside, usually tied to a mover or to daylight. A homeowner fix for a damp block wall might be a single repair and a coating. All three are waterproofing systems; they just treat different amounts of water.
          </p>

          <p>
            This matters because quotes tend to bundle everything. A contractor might propose an interior drain, a wall membrane, a premium pump, a battery backup, a dehumidifier and crack repairs as one package. Some of those parts may be exactly right for your house. Others may be there because they come with the package. Breaking the quote into parts lets you ask why each one is needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before any of these, the outer defenses come first: gutters, downspout extensions and grading. They are not part of any branded system because there is little money in them, but they decide how hard the system has to work. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-waterproof-a-basement/">how to waterproof a basement</a> walks through those steps in order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="match" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Match the System to the Symptom
          </h2>

          <p>
            The symptom tells you which part of a system you need. Water that appears as a dark band or seepage on the wall face points to a barrier problem: the concrete is letting water through. Water along the seam where the wall meets the floor points to a collection problem: saturated soil around the footing is pushing water up through the joint. Water through the middle of the slab points to a high water table.
          </p>

          <p>
            Condensation is the symptom no waterproofing system fixes. If a plastic sheet taped to the wall collects droplets on the room side, the moisture is in the air. A dehumidifier and better ventilation handle that for a few hundred dollars. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">fixing a damp basement</a> explains the test and the fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Location matters as well. Water coming in on one wall only, often the uphill side or the side with a downspout, suggests an outdoor drainage fix or a partial system on that wall. Water all around the perimeter suggests a full drain. Water around a single crack calls for crack injection rather than any system at all.
          </p>

