        <div id="interior-drain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Interior Perimeter Drains: The Workhorse System
          </h2>

          <p>
            An interior perimeter drain, sometimes called interior drain tile or a French drain inside the basement, is the most common professional system for good reason. It does not try to stop water from reaching the foundation. It intercepts water at the footing, where it would otherwise rise through the floor joint, and carries it to a sump pit where a pump sends it outside.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installation follows a consistent pattern. The crew breaks out a strip of floor about a foot wide along the walls, digs down beside the footing, lays perforated pipe in clean stone, and slopes it to a sump pit. On block walls, small holes are often drilled in the bottom course so water trapped in the hollow cores can drain into the trench. A dimpled plastic sheet is usually fastened to the wall and tucked into the trench before the floor is patched with new concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            The advantages are practical. It can be installed year-round from inside without disturbing landscaping, decks or driveways, it handles water from the wall and from under the slab, and it costs much less than excavating the whole foundation. It is also the right answer for older homes whose original exterior footing drains have clogged or collapsed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The disadvantage is that the wall still gets wet; you are managing water rather than stopping it. A wall membrane keeps that water out of sight and directs it down, but it also hides the wall surface. If you plan to finish the basement, that is usually fine. If you prefer to see and monitor the foundation, ask for a clear membrane or a system that leaves the wall exposed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="exterior" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Exterior Membranes and Footing Drains
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior waterproofing attacks the problem at its source. The foundation is excavated to the footing, cleaned and repaired, then coated with a waterproofing membrane: a spray or roll-on rubberized product, a self-adhered sheet, or a bentonite clay panel system. Drainage board goes over the membrane to relieve pressure, and a perforated footing drain in gravel carries water away before backfill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because water hits the membrane first, pressure pushes the membrane against the wall instead of pulling it off. That is why exterior work is called positive-side waterproofing and why it is considered the most complete fix. It also keeps the concrete itself dry, which matters for block walls that freeze and thaw and for foundations with steel reinforcement close to the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distinguish waterproofing from damp-proofing. Many houses were built with a thin asphalt spray on the outside of the foundation, which building codes accept as damp-proofing. It slows moisture but is not designed to resist water under pressure and becomes brittle with age. If a contractor says your house was waterproofed when built, ask whether that was a true membrane or just damp-proofing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The downside is cost and disruption. Excavation means removing landscaping, sometimes porches, steps or patios, and digging 7 to 9 feet down beside the house. It is often done one wall at a time where the leak is concentrated. If you are already digging for a sewer repair, an addition or structural wall work, adding a membrane while the wall is exposed is excellent value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Exterior work also gives you a chance to fix the conditions that caused the problem. While the soil is out, the crew can replace clogged footing drains, correct backfill that holds water against the wall, and regrade so the finished surface slopes away. Ask that the backfill be free-draining gravel or clean soil rather than the clay that was dug out, which can put the wall right back under the same pressure.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">DAMP-PROOFING IS NOT WATERPROOFING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check what is really on the outside of your wall.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The thin black coating on many foundations is damp-proofing, which slows vapor but is not built to stop water under pressure. A true waterproofing membrane is thicker, flexible and installed with drainage board.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Choosing a Sump Pump That Will Last
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pump is the part most likely to fail, so it deserves more attention than it gets. For most homes, a 1/3 horsepower submersible pump moves enough water; homes with high water tables, long or high discharge runs, or very large drainage areas may need 1/2 horsepower. Bigger is not automatically better, because an oversized pump short-cycles in a small pit and wears its switch out sooner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Construction matters more than horsepower. Cast-iron housings shed motor heat into the water and hold up for years; thermoplastic pumps are cheaper and lighter but generally shorter-lived under heavy use. A mechanical float switch, either vertical or tethered, is the dependable standard. Vertical floats suit narrow pits; tethered floats need room to swing and can snag on the pit wall if the pit is too small.
          </p>

          <p>
            The discharge line matters too. Use a check valve to keep water from draining back into the pit after each cycle, drill a small weep hole in the pipe between the pump and the check valve if the manufacturer recommends it, and carry discharge far enough from the house that it does not soak the soil next to the foundation. Many municipalities prohibit sending sump water into the sanitary sewer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Sump Pump Rules of Thumb</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/3 HP</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Enough for most homes</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">7-10 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical pump service life</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">18-24 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common pit diameter</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Check valve on every discharge</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Expect to spend roughly $150 to $300 on a quality cast-iron primary pump at the time of writing, plus fittings. Replacing a pump in an existing pit is a manageable DIY job for most owners. Installing a new pit means cutting the slab and should be planned alongside the drain that feeds it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="backup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Battery and Water-Powered Backup Pumps
          </h2>

          <p>
            A primary pump without a backup is a single point of failure, and it tends to fail at the worst moment: during a storm that has knocked out power while the pit is filling fastest. A backup pump sits in the same pit, slightly higher than the primary, and takes over if the primary stops or cannot keep up. Most include an alarm so you know something is wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery backups are the most common. A 12-volt pump runs from a deep-cycle battery kept charged by a controller plugged into the wall. Run time depends on battery capacity and how often the pump cycles; manufacturers typically list estimates in hours of continuous pumping or number of cycles. Plan on replacing the battery every few years, and choose a sealed AGM battery if you want less maintenance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-powered backups use municipal water pressure to create suction that empties the pit, with no battery to wear out. They need city water with adequate pressure, and local plumbing codes typically require a backflow preventer, so check before buying. They are not an option on a private well, because a power outage also stops the well pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            Combination units pair a primary and a battery backup in one assembly with a shared discharge, which simplifies installation in a single pit. A smart controller or Wi-Fi alarm that texts you when the pump fails or the water rises is inexpensive insurance if the house is often empty. Whatever you choose, test the backup by unplugging the primary and filling the pit with a bucket a few times a year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="crack-coat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Crack Injection and Wall Coatings as Part of a System
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every leaking basement needs a drain. A poured concrete wall that leaks at a single shrinkage crack is usually fixed by injecting the crack. Polyurethane resin expands to fill the crack through the full wall thickness and stays flexible; epoxy bonds the two sides structurally. A DIY kit costs a fraction of a contractor visit and, done carefully, can be a permanent fix for a non-structural crack.
          </p>

          <p>
            Injection works best on poured concrete. Block walls are hollow, so resin can disappear into the cores rather than sealing the crack, and cracks in block tend to follow mortar joints. For block, a combination of joint repair, coating and, if water still enters at the floor, an interior drain is usually more effective.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wall coatings fill the barrier role from the inside. A masonry waterproofer brushed onto bare block or concrete reduces wicking and slow seepage and is often all a damp wall needs once outdoor drainage is fixed. Our ranking of the <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">best basement wall sealers</a> covers which coating to choose; for hollow block in particular, see our list of the best <a href="/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/">cinder block sealers</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coatings and drains also work together. With an interior drain installed, a coating on the walls above it reduces the amount of water that reaches the membrane and keeps the visible portion of the wall drier. Coatings applied before a drain is installed, on a wall with water pouring through the floor joint, tend to look like failures when the real problem was never the wall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="foundation-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Foundation Type Changes the Right System
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Poured concrete</strong> walls are solid and fairly watertight between cracks, so many leaks in poured basements come through a handful of shrinkage cracks, form-tie holes and the floor joint. That makes them good candidates for targeted repairs: crack injection, tie-hole plugs and a penetrating sealer or coating where the wall is damp. A full drain is usually only needed when water rises along the floor edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Concrete block</strong> walls behave differently. Each block has hollow cores that can fill with water, and every mortar joint is a potential path. Water often enters the cores high on the wall and runs down inside to the bottom course, where it seeps out along the floor. That is why interior drains for block walls usually include weep holes drilled into the lowest course, and why block basements more often end up with a full drain.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stone and brick</strong> foundations in older homes were never designed to be watertight. Mortar erodes, and water moves through the joints readily. Coatings tend to trap moisture in these walls and can damage soft historic mortar, so the usual approach is drainage plus repointing with a compatible mortar rather than sealing the face. Get advice from a contractor familiar with older masonry before applying anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Slab-on-grade houses and crawl spaces have their own systems, such as crawl space encapsulation with a heavy vapor barrier, which are outside the scope of this page. If your home has a partial basement next to a crawl space, though, water from the crawl space can migrate into the basement, so look at both areas when tracing a leak.
          </p>
        </div>

